# Ossie interchange in GND

Apache Ossie is an open source project that defines a file format for
ontologies, meaning the business concepts in a domain and the relationships
between them. If an Ossie ontology already exists for your domain, you can
import it and use its concepts and relationships as the starting point for a
GND model. If your model lives in GND, as the refunds example does, you can
export its vocabulary and relationships so that another tool or team works
from the same names and connections.

GND supports Ossie ontology version `0.3.0.dev0`. The export schema and
validation tool come from Apache Ossie
`ee89dbdc2844d8b7d293ed42c8613928bb99505f`. The parser used for the
export check comes from Apache Ossie Converters
`83ddc294c8840f5f497a2bdbec2348a128fc2254`.

An export covers three Ossie layers. The **ontology** holds concepts, their
relationships and a documented subset of constraint formulas. The **semantic
model** describes tables that hold the model's facts. The **mapping** says
which table and column holds each concept and relationship. Import reads
the ontology only. Ossie's metrics, dimensions and query interfaces are
outside this scope.

!!! info "Files on GitHub"

    [Normative mapping and file format](https://github.com/vasiloglou/gnd-public/blob/main/src/gnd/skills/gnd-orm-core/references/representation-format.md#ossie-interchange) ·
    [Relational mapping rules](https://github.com/vasiloglou/gnd-public/blob/main/src/gnd/skills/gnd-orm-core/references/representation-format.md#relational-mapping-rmap)

This page explains what the export does and why. The rules themselves have
one home, the two sections linked above.

## Share an ontology and keep the original details

Use `gnd export ossie MODEL --out ONTOLOGY.yaml` to share the ontology.
The sidecar preserves GND details, and the loss report shows how each
source field was handled. Keep the sidecar beside the ontology for a
GND round trip. `gnd import ossie ONTOLOGY.yaml --out MODEL.json` restores
those details when the sidecar is present. What the export omitted stays
omitted: a model with example populations comes back without them. A foreign
ontology imports through the supported projection and gets its own loss report.

An export is the model's own text. A fact type or role marked `redact` keeps
its reading, constraints and provenance text in the export, in every
[disclosure mode](../reference/disclosure.md): `redact` withholds population
values from reports, and an export carries no population. The command prints a
note with the number of fact types that declare `redact`, so you can decide
before you share the files.

GND keeps its `Duration` type, which counts whole days. Ossie receives an
integer value type and the sidecar preserves its original GND meaning.
The export report also records omissions, such as example populations.
Use `--strict` when any refusal should stop the export.

## The tables an export describes

A GND model states facts: a refund is requested by a customer, a refund is
for an amount. A database stores facts in tables. The export works out a set
of tables for the model and writes them as the semantic model.

The method is the standard one for this kind of model:

- A business object that has facts of its own gets a table. Its key is the
  way the business identifies it, such as a refund number.
- A fact that can hold only once per object becomes a column of that
  object's table. If each refund has one requesting customer, the customer
  is a column of the refund table.
- A fact that can hold many times gets a table of its own, linked back by a
  foreign key.
- A uniqueness rule that fits inside one table becomes a key of that table.

The result depends only on the model, so two exports of the same model give
the same bytes.

These tables are GND's reading of the model, not tables anyone has built.
Each one carries the marker `"source_kind":"logical"`, so that a reader does
not take its name for a real table in a warehouse. The semantic model and
the mapping are written beside the ontology, as `OUT.semantic_model.<fmt>`
and `OUT.mapping.<fmt>`. Add `--ontology-only` to write the ontology, sidecar
and loss report without them.

GND checks the semantic model and the mapping against the pinned Ossie
schemas before it writes them.

## What is listed instead of mapped

Tables carry facts, keys and links. They do not carry every rule, and a few
shapes do not fit Ossie's mapping format. Nothing is dropped silently. The
mapping file holds a list, under its `GND` extension, of every fact type and
every constraint in the model. Each entry says where it is carried, or gives
a reason it is not. The reasons you are most likely to meet:

| Reason | What it means |
|---|---|
| `not-relational` | A rule over values or across facts, such as a spending limit. A table definition cannot state it. It stays in the sidecar. |
| `uniqueness-not-confirmed` | A uniqueness rule that is not yet confirmed in the model, so it is not written as a key. |
| `spans-tables` | A rule whose facts ended up in more than one table. |
| `derived-computed` | A fact that is worked out from others and not stored. |
| `identification-differs` | The ontology and the tables identify an object in different ways, so the link between them is not written. |
| `arity-over-limit` | A fact type with more than 32 roles. It keeps its table, and the mapping does not link it. |

Three limits follow from the same principle of listing and not guessing:

- A model that yields no table at all writes the ontology's three files and
  prints a note, because an Ossie semantic model needs at least one table.
- An object identified through a chain of more than 8 other objects is
  treated as one the ontology does not identify.
- A fact type with more than 32 roles is listed, as the table shows.

## Describe the tables you delivered

The tables above are a proposal. If you have already built your schema, with
your own table and column names, give the export a **binding file** with
`--physical-binding FILE`. The binding lists your tables and says which
columns hold which roles of the model. The semantic model then describes
your tables, marked `"source_kind":"physical"`, and the mapping points at
your columns.

A binding does not need to cover the whole model. Fact types it does not
mention are listed with the reason `unbound`. A binding that contradicts the
model, for example by naming a role the fact type does not have, is refused
with a rule id that starts with `binding:`, and nothing is written.

One limit matters when you plan a binding: a role has to be bound to
columns that hold the model's own identifier for the object. A fact that a
table holds only through a generated surrogate key cannot be bound. It is
left out and listed as `unbound`, while the table and its foreign key are
still described.

Without the option, an export is exactly what it was.

- [Describe your delivered tables](../how-to/describe-delivered-tables-for-ossie.md)

## Large models

Each written file may be at most 10 MiB by default. A large model, such as
one composed from many shared vocabularies, can exceed that. The export then
stops with `ossie:output-size` and writes nothing. Raise the bound for that
run with `--max-output-bytes N`, up to 256 MiB.

Import has the matching option. `gnd import ossie` reads documents up to
10 MiB unless `--max-input-bytes N` sets another bound.

Neither option changes the limit on the model file itself, which stays at
10 MiB.

## Where PyRel fits

PyRel is optional. You can export and import using GND alone. The offline
check used the same Ossie Converters commit with RelationalAI 1.39.0.
It generated PyRel source for 36 of the 38 eligible exports. Concept
names, parent links, relationship names and identifier links matched the
exported declarations. The converter refused one inherited identifier,
and the SDK could not generate source for one derivation chain. The
emitted source contained no `require` calls.

GND can generate its own PyRel rules for supported models. The formula
test corpus compares GND checker results with hand-authored verdicts,
and the Ossie parser checks the corresponding formula syntax. Executing
Ossie formulas is outside the scope of those checks.

- [Share the refunds example](../how-to/share-an-ossie-ontology.md)
- [What PyRel is, and when you need it](what-is-pyrel.md)
# Share a refund model as an Ossie ontology

Your team records who requested each refund, how much it was for, and how
it was paid. A partner wants the same vocabulary for their reporting tool.
Share the ontology so both teams use the same relationships, and the tables
that go with it so the partner can see where each fact would be stored. Keep
the sidecar when a GND user needs to recover the original rules.

!!! info "Files on GitHub"

    [Refunds example model](https://github.com/vasiloglou/gnd-public/blob/main/src/gnd/examples/refunds/model.orm.yaml)

With the `/gnd` skill, give it this prompt:

> Export the refunds example as an Ossie ontology with its tables. Show how
> the output represents who requested a refund, and which table holds it.
> Then import it and compare the restored model with the original.

## Export the model

The refunds example ships inside GND. The first command copies it into a
`refunds` folder in your working directory. The second writes the export.
Use a new output name for another run.

<!-- gnd-run expect=0 -->
```sh
python -c "import gnd, pathlib, shutil; shutil.copytree(pathlib.Path(gnd.__file__).parent / 'examples/refunds', 'refunds', dirs_exist_ok=True)"
gnd export ossie refunds/model.orm.yaml --out refunds.ossie.yaml
```

The export writes five files:

<!-- gnd-run expect=0 output=stdout -->
```sh
ls refunds.ossie.yaml*
```

```text
refunds.ossie.yaml
refunds.ossie.yaml.gnd.json
refunds.ossie.yaml.loss.json
refunds.ossie.yaml.mapping.yaml
refunds.ossie.yaml.semantic_model.yaml
```

| File | What it gives you |
|---|---|
| `refunds.ossie.yaml` | The ontology you can share with an Ossie reader. |
| `refunds.ossie.yaml.semantic_model.yaml` | The tables, columns, keys and links between tables that would hold the model's facts. |
| `refunds.ossie.yaml.mapping.yaml` | Which table and column holds each concept and relationship of the ontology. |
| `refunds.ossie.yaml.gnd.json` | The GND details needed to restore the original model, including the policy about refund amounts. |
| `refunds.ossie.yaml.loss.json` | A list of source fields and how the export handled each one. |

## Read the relationship

The output relationship `refund_by` is many-to-one. One customer can
request several refunds. Each refund has at most one requesting customer.

<!-- gnd-run expect=0 output=stdout -->
```sh
sed -n '10,16p' refunds.ossie.yaml
```

```text
  - name: refund_by
    verbalizes:
    - '{Refund} is requested by {Customer:customer}'
    roles:
    - concept: Customer
      name: customer
    multiplicity: ManyToOne
```

![Each refund has at most one requesting customer.](../assets/applications/refund-requester.svg)

## See the tables

Because each refund has one requesting customer, the customer sits in a
column of the refund's own table. The table's key is the refund number.

<!-- gnd-run expect=0 output=stdout -->
```sh
sed -n '6,18p' refunds.ossie.yaml.semantic_model.yaml
```

```text
- name: refund
  source: refund
  primary_key:
  - refund_no
  fields:
  - name: refund_no
    expression:
      sql: '"REFUND_NO"'
    datatype: Integer
  - name: customer_customer_code
    expression:
      sql: '"CUSTOMER_CUSTOMER_CODE"'
    datatype: String
```

The model does not say that a refund has only one amount or one payment
channel. So those two facts each get a table of their own, `refund_amount`
and `refund_channel`, linked back to `refund`. If your business has one
amount per refund, say so in the model and the amount moves into the refund
table.

These tables are GND's reading of the model. They are not tables you have
built, and each one is marked `"source_kind":"logical"` to say so. To
describe the tables you did build, see
[Describe your delivered tables](describe-delivered-tables-for-ossie.md).

## Check what the tables do not carry

Tables can hold facts and keys. They cannot hold every rule. The mapping
file lists each fact type and rule of the model with where it is carried, or
the reason it is not. This command prints the ones that are not:

<!-- gnd-run expect=0 output=stdout -->
```sh
grep -o '"id":"[a-z_/0-9]*","kind":"[a-z_]*","reason":"[a-z-]*"' refunds.ossie.yaml.mapping.yaml
```

```text
"id":"constraints/0","kind":"constraint","reason":"not-relational"
```

That one entry is the policy about refund amounts. It is a rule over values,
which a table definition cannot express, so it travels in the sidecar. The
[Ossie explanation](../explanation/ossie.md#what-is-listed-instead-of-mapped)
describes the reasons you can meet.

## Restore the model

<!-- gnd-run expect=0 -->
```sh
gnd import ossie refunds.ossie.yaml --out restored.orm.json
gnd diff refunds/model.orm.yaml restored.orm.json
```

| File | What it gives you |
|---|---|
| `restored.orm.json` | The restored GND model. |
| `restored.orm.json.loss.json` | The import report, including whether the restored model matched the source identity. |

The diff reports the same model identity for both files and compares
each of the two rules in both directions. For a GND restore, keep the
sidecar and loss report with an exact copy of the original ontology.
Reformatting the ontology changes the bytes the sidecar verifies. Import
reads the ontology and its two companions. It does not read the semantic
model or the mapping.

## Share the ontology alone

If your partner wants only the vocabulary, add `--ontology-only`. The run
writes the ontology, the sidecar and the loss report, as GND did before it
wrote tables.

<!-- gnd-run expect=0 output=stdout -->
```sh
gnd export ossie refunds/model.orm.yaml --out vocabulary.yaml --ontology-only >/dev/null
ls vocabulary.yaml*
```

```text
vocabulary.yaml
vocabulary.yaml.gnd.json
vocabulary.yaml.loss.json
```

An export never overwrites a file. Running the first export again stops with
exit code 11 and writes nothing, because its files already exist:

<!-- gnd-run expect=11 -->
```sh
gnd export ossie refunds/model.orm.yaml --out refunds.ossie.yaml
```

## Before you share another model

Check the loss report and the mapping's list before sharing. For example, a
model's example data is omitted and listed as refused. Add `--strict` to stop
the export if any source field would be refused. A very large model can
exceed the size limit of a file; the
[explanation](../explanation/ossie.md#large-models) says how to raise it.

[Read the Ossie interchange scope](../explanation/ossie.md).
# Describe your delivered tables in an Ossie export

Your warehouse already has a refunds table. Its name and columns are not the
ones GND would choose. You want the Ossie files to describe the table your
partner will actually query, and to say which of its columns holds each fact
of the model.

A **binding file** does that. It lists your tables and says which columns
hold which roles. With it, the export describes your tables. Without it,
nothing about the export changes.

!!! info "Files on GitHub"

    [Example binding](https://github.com/vasiloglou/gnd-public/blob/main/src/gnd/examples/refunds/physical-binding.json) ·
    [Binding schema](https://github.com/vasiloglou/gnd-public/blob/main/src/gnd/schema/physical-binding.schema.json) ·
    [Binding rules](https://github.com/vasiloglou/gnd-public/blob/main/src/gnd/skills/gnd-orm-core/references/representation-format.md#physical-binding)

With the `/gnd` skill, give it this prompt:

> Export the refunds example as an Ossie ontology, described by the tables
> in its example binding. Show me the refunds table it writes and list
> anything in the model the binding leaves out.

## Read the binding

The refunds example ships inside GND with a binding for a small warehouse.
The first command copies the example into a `refunds` folder. The second
prints the binding.

<!-- gnd-run expect=0 output=stdout -->
```sh
python -c "import gnd, pathlib, shutil; shutil.copytree(pathlib.Path(gnd.__file__).parent / 'examples/refunds', 'refunds', dirs_exist_ok=True)"
cat refunds/physical-binding.json
```

```text
{
  "schema": "gnd-physical-binding/1",
  "tables": {
    "crm.customers": {
      "columns": {
        "cust_code": {
          "sql_type": "VARCHAR(12)",
          "targets": [{"kind": "identifier", "object_type": "customer"}]
        }
      },
      "primary_key": ["cust_code"]
    },
    "fin.refunds": {
      "columns": {
        "refund_id": {
          "sql_type": "BIGINT",
          "targets": [{"kind": "identifier", "object_type": "refund"}]
        },
        "cust_code": {
          "sql_type": "VARCHAR(12)",
          "references": {"table": "crm.customers", "columns": ["cust_code"]}
        },
        "amount_cents": {"sql_type": "INTEGER"},
        "pay_channel": {"sql_type": "VARCHAR(20)"},
        "loaded_at": {"sql_type": "TIMESTAMP"}
      },
      "primary_key": ["refund_id"]
    }
  },
  "roles": {
    "refund_by": {
      "refund": ["fin.refunds.refund_id"],
      "customer": ["fin.refunds.cust_code"]
    },
    "refund_amount": {
      "refund": ["fin.refunds.refund_id"],
      "amount": ["fin.refunds.amount_cents"]
    }
  }
}
```

It has two parts:

- **`tables`** is your schema: each table, its columns in order, their SQL
  types, the primary key, and each foreign key as a `references` entry. A
  column that holds the key of a business object says so with an
  `identifier` target. Here `refund_id` identifies a refund.
- **`roles`** connects the model to those columns. For each fact type, each
  role names the column that holds it. `refund_by` says the refund is in
  `refund_id` and the requesting customer is in `cust_code`.

A column the model knows nothing about, such as `loaded_at`, needs no
entry in `roles`. It is still described as part of the table.

## Export with the binding

<!-- gnd-run expect=0 -->
```sh
gnd export ossie refunds/model.orm.yaml --out warehouse.ossie.yaml --physical-binding refunds/physical-binding.json
```

The semantic model now describes your tables, under your names, and the
foreign key becomes one link between them:

<!-- gnd-run expect=0 output=stdout -->
```sh
tail -9 warehouse.ossie.yaml.semantic_model.yaml
```

```text
    data: '{"source_kind":"physical"}'
relationships:
- name: fin.refunds__crm.customers
  from: fin.refunds
  to: crm.customers
  from_columns:
  - cust_code
  to_columns:
  - cust_code
```

Each table is marked `"source_kind":"physical"`: it is a table you
delivered, not one GND proposed. The ontology, the sidecar and the loss
report are the same files, byte for byte, as an export without the binding.

## See what the binding leaves out

The binding does not have to cover the whole model. The mapping file lists
whatever it leaves out, with a reason:

<!-- gnd-run expect=0 output=stdout -->
```sh
grep -o '"id":"[a-z_/0-9]*","kind":"[a-z_]*","reason":"[a-z-]*"' warehouse.ossie.yaml.mapping.yaml
```

```text
"id":"constraints/0","kind":"constraint","reason":"not-relational"
"id":"refund_channel","kind":"fact_type","reason":"unbound"
```

- `refund_channel` is `unbound`: the binding names a `pay_channel` column
  but never says which fact it holds. To fix it, add this entry under
  `roles` and export again under a new name:

    ```json
    "refund_channel": {
      "refund": ["fin.refunds.refund_id"],
      "channel": ["fin.refunds.pay_channel"]
    }
    ```

- The policy about refund amounts is `not-relational`, as it is without a
  binding. No table definition can carry it.

The mapping also records the SHA-256 of the binding file, so a reader can
tell which binding an export was made from.

## When a binding is refused

GND checks the binding against the model before it writes anything. A
binding that names something the model does not have is refused. This
command makes a copy with a misspelled role name and tries it:

<!-- gnd-run expect=3 output=stderr -->
```sh
sed 's/"customer":/"custmer":/' refunds/physical-binding.json > broken.json
gnd export ossie refunds/model.orm.yaml --out broken.ossie.yaml --physical-binding broken.json
```

```text
invalid Ossie export input: binding:unknown-role: refund_by has no role 'custmer'
```

The run exits with code 3 and writes no file. The message starts with a rule
id, here `binding:unknown-role`. Fix the binding and run again. Do not
change the model to fit a binding. The
[binding rules](https://github.com/vasiloglou/gnd-public/blob/main/src/gnd/skills/gnd-orm-core/references/representation-format.md#physical-binding)
list every rule id and the order GND checks them in.

## Limits

- **Surrogate keys.** A role has to be bound to columns that hold the
  model's own identifier for the object, such as the refund number. A fact
  that a table holds only through a generated id cannot be bound. Leave it
  out of `roles` and it is listed as `unbound`. The table and its foreign
  key are still described.
- **Size.** A binding file can be at most 10 MiB.
- **Not with `--ontology-only`.** That option writes no tables, so the two
  together are a usage error.

[Read the Ossie interchange scope](../explanation/ossie.md).
---
name: gnd-export
description: Exports and imports pinned Apache Ossie ontologies with loss reports, and exports their semantic model and mapping, optionally over delivered tables. Exports finite Lean models with unproved obligations, finite grounded witnesses, or explicitly selected bounded standalone Souffle derivation programs. Native compilation/execution and positive proof replay are separate optional claims; never source truth, completeness or assurance.
---

# Derivation program and finite witness export

Read the [export boundary](references/export-contract.md) and
[certificate contract](references/witness-contract.md). The closed
[certificate schema](references/witness.schema.json) pins versions 1 and 2.
Version 2 is for a population made by `gnd compose`: add `--composition RECORD`
to both `gnd witness` and `gnd replay`, as the certificate contract describes.

1. Select the model, candidate population, fixed-reference root and a nonempty
   version-1 target request. Preserve exact input bytes.
2. Run `gnd witness MODEL POP --targets REQUEST --reference-root ROOT` for a
   redacted, non-replayable view. Inspect every request's status. For a
   `missing` row, write a one-target request for that row and run
   `gnd why-not MODEL POP --fact-type ID --row ROW_REQUEST --reference-root ROOT`.
   It names the first rule step that left no bindings; it explains, it proves
   nothing. Its atom blockers count rows spelled differently (`near_miss_count`);
   read the pairs with `--raw` before changing a spelling.
3. On explicit raw export selection, run the same command with
   `--raw --out NEW_CERTIFICATE`. The destination is create-once; raw certificates
   contain source values. Never overwrite an existing destination. Under
   `GND_DISCLOSURE=customer` this is refused with exit 3; do not retry it in
   another mode for that reader.
4. Replay separately with `gnd replay MODEL POP CERTIFICATE --reference-root ROOT --json`.
   Exit zero means every requested finite proof replayed against those bound
   inputs. Missing or unknown results establish no absence theorem.

For an explicitly selected standalone Souffle program, follow the export boundary
above: render offline, verify its bound bundle, then select native execution or
positive provenance separately. Raw publication requires explicit `--raw`.

This skill never touches git. It never certifies, starts another skill,
regenerates code, automatically invokes an optional solver, or widens the fragment.
For population input representation, consult
[ORM core](../gnd-orm-core/SKILL.md). Each claim stays separate.

When the owner requests a development record, use the
[milestone record contract](../gnd-orm-core/references/milestone-record-format.md).
The driving agent owns that record and commit; GND never touches git.

`gnd export ilp` is not a derivation export. It is the ILP bridge: it writes
labelled populations for a rule learner with `--target` `popper`, `neutral`,
`ilasp` or `rules`, and `gnd import ilp-rules` brings the learner's rules back
as an evolve proposal (`--target llm` reads an agent's JSON answer). With
`--target` `amie` it writes one unlabelled population for a rule miner, and
`gnd import mined-rules` brings back only rules that hold on it. See
`gnd export ilp --help`; the proposal is reviewed like any other evolution
proposal.

For explicitly selected Lean definitions, run `gnd export lean MODEL POP
--toolchain leanprover/lean4:v4.34.0` offline. Publish a raw create-once bundle
only with `--raw --out NEW_DIR`, then run `gnd export lean-verify NEW_DIR --json`.
Compilation is separately selected with `gnd export lean-compile NEW_DIR
--executable LAKE --json`; `--elaborate-stubs` records stub elaboration separately.
Neither operation proves a theorem. The selected positive acyclic finite profile,
refusals, exact propositions and ceilings are in the export boundary above.

## Ossie interchange

Use `gnd export ossie MODEL --out ONTOLOGY.yaml` to create five files: the
ontology, the `ONTOLOGY.yaml.gnd.json` sidecar, the
`ONTOLOGY.yaml.loss.json` loss report (move it with `--loss-report`), and the
relational pair `ONTOLOGY.yaml.semantic_model.yaml` and
`ONTOLOGY.yaml.mapping.yaml`. `--format json` writes JSON instead. Add
`--ontology-only` when the user wants the ontology without tables; the run
then writes the first three files.

Before sharing, inspect two lists. The loss report names refused pointers.
The mapping's `GND` extension lists every fact type and constraint under
`dispositions`, each carried or given a reason, and may hold
`unmapped_links`. Report the entries that carry a reason; never describe
them as mapped. Use `--strict` when a refusal should prevent writing the
bundle.

When the user has delivered tables, pass their binding file with
`--physical-binding FILE`. The semantic model then describes those tables.
A refused binding exits 3 with a `binding:` rule id and writes nothing.
Repair the binding. Never edit the model to make a binding pass, and do not
combine the option with `--ontology-only`.

An export stopped by `ossie:output-size` can be rerun with
`--max-output-bytes N`. Use `--max-input-bytes N` on import for a document
above the default bound. Raise a bound only for a size refusal that names
it, never to get past a different refusal. Outputs are create-once: an
existing file at any output path exits 11.

Keep the ontology, sidecar and loss report together for a GND round trip
with `gnd import ossie ONTOLOGY.yaml --out MODEL.json`. Preserve the
ontology bytes so import can verify the sidecar binding. Import reads the
ontology only, not the relational pair. The import report is written to
`MODEL.json.loss.json` by default, or to the path given by `--loss-report`.

Use the [normative Ossie mapping and file format](../gnd-orm-core/references/representation-format.md#ossie-interchange)
for supported declarations, pins, output formats, the relational files, the
binding rules and import rules.
The ordinary CLI routes run offline. Select any external consumer
verification separately.

